<GuidePage title='Bootstrapping the First Paint - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 6 · Part A: Maximize the Known</div>
  <h1>Bootstrapping the First Paint</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Some views hinge on state that doesn't fit in a URL. That is not a license to client-fetch them
    behind a skeleton: the server can still source the data and paint the page complete, with the
    client's own fetching deferred until the browser is idle. This chapter is that pattern, where it
    came from, and the honest caveat about when the App Router has a better answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id='problem'>6.1 The gap this fills</h2>
  <p>
    Part A's preferred shape is simple: put the view's inputs in the URL, resolve them on the
    server, render finished HTML (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>). But every real
    application has a few views where that gets hard. Think of a large data table wearing every
    feature at once: composite filters, free-text search, multi-column ordering, cursor-based
    pagination, per-user view preferences. Some of that state is expressible in a URL and should be;
    some of it is genuinely awkward there, whether because the state is too rich to serialize
    cleanly, too transient to deserve history entries, or owned by client machinery (a grid library,
    a saved-views system) that was never going to read <code>searchParams</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The common surrender at that point is to hand the whole view to the client: fetch in a hook,
    paint a skeleton first, content later. And that surrender gives up the one thing this entire
    part of the guide exists to protect, the complete first paint. There is a middle path, and it
    predates the App Router: <strong>bootstrap the view from the server, and defer the client's own
    fetching until after the paint</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id='pattern'>6.2 The pattern: server seeds, client validates on idle</h2>
  <p>The pattern has three moves, and the order is the point. <Chip k='react' /><Chip k='very' /></p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The server sources the data during the request.</strong> In the pages router this was
      <code>getServerSideProps</code>; in the App Router it is simply a server component awaiting
      the query. Either way, the initial view's data arrives as a prop, and the page renders
      <em>everything</em> on the first paint: the table, its rows, its controls. No spinners, no
      skeletons, nothing to swap.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The client's twin query exists but starts paused.</strong> The same data has a client
      hook (SWR, TanStack Query, urql), because interactions will need it later, but on load it is
      gated behind an idle signal: <code>requestIdleCallback</code> with a timeout fallback. The
      second fetch never competes with the main thread while the page renders and hydrates; it fires
      a few seconds later, quietly, because nothing needs it immediately.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Once awake, the client copy takes over.</strong> The component renders
      <code>clientData ?? bootstrappedData</code>: the deferred fetch self-validates against the
      server copy (catching accidental mismatches), and from then on the data is a live citizen of
      the client cache. When state changes (a filter, a mutation, an invalidation), the hook refires
      on its own, so everything born from an interaction refreshes client-side without ceremony.
    </li>
  </ol>

