  <h2 id='mechanics'>6.3 The mechanics, in code</h2>
  <p>
    The idle gate is a few lines. The browser's own scheduler says when the main thread has settled;
    a timeout guarantees it fires even on a busy page:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">hooks/use-is-idle.ts - "the browser has settled down"</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">useIsIdle</span>({ timeout = <span class="tok-str">2000</span> } = {}) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isIdle, setIsIdle] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);
  <span class="tok-fn">useEffect</span>(() =&gt; {
    <span class="tok-kw">const</span> handle = window.<span class="tok-fn">requestIdleCallback</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setIsIdle</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>), { timeout });
    <span class="tok-kw">return</span> () =&gt; window.<span class="tok-fn">cancelIdleCallback</span>(handle);
  }, [timeout]);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> isIdle;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The production version adds a <code>setTimeout</code> fallback for browsers without <code
        >requestIdleCallback</code
      > and a cancellation guard, but this is the whole idea: a boolean that flips once the browser has
      nothing better to do (or the timeout passes), used to hold a query back from the critical path.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The provider is where the three moves meet. This is the shape <code>ojl-tracker</code> uses for
    both the authenticated user and the program model: seeded from a server prop, refreshed by an
    idle-deferred query, live thereafter:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramProvider.tsx - seeded by the server, validated on idle, live afterward</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramProvider</span>({ bootstrappedProgram, programId, children }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> isIdle = <span class="tok-fn">useIsIdle</span>();

  <span class="tok-com">/* Paused until idle: this request never competes with the first render.
     Once it runs it stays live, so cache invalidation and state changes
     refresh the program for the whole subtree. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>({ programId }, { <span class="tok-prop">pause</span>: !isIdle });

  <span class="tok-com">/* The client copy wins once it exists; until then, the server copy
     already painted everything. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = data ?? bootstrappedProgram;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramContext</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={program}&gt;{children}&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">ProgramContext</span>&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Straight from the real pattern: the authenticated-user version defers five seconds and relies
      on the query staying subscribed so profile edits during the session refresh the user in
      context automatically. One practical detail worth copying: compare the two copies with a
      deep-equality memo before swapping, so a byte-identical client response doesn't re-render the
      subtree for nothing.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>


  <h2 id='auth-chain'>6.4 Case study: the auth chain that blocked every page</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest real instance of this pattern isn't a table at all; it's authentication. Every hard
    navigation in the <code>ojl-tracker</code> app used to render <em>nothing</em> until a serial
    chain of client-side providers resolved: first the Auth0 client asked <code>/api/auth/me</code>
    who the user was; only after that resolved did the authenticated-user GraphQL query fire; only
    after <em>that</em> (plus a client-environment fetch) did the feature-flag provider initialize
    against its CDN. Each gate rendered a full-page loading screen while it waited, and each waited
    on the previous, so a cold load spent seconds inside the chain before the page shell appeared:
    hard-navigation TTI ran seconds long while soft navigations took a few hundred milliseconds, a
    split that averages quietly hide. It is <a href='13-blocking-requests.html#csr-depth'>§13.5</a>'s
    serial-by-structure waterfall, wearing auth clothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tell, and the reason this is a bootstrap case study, is that
    <strong>every fact in that chain was already known to the server.</strong> The session is
    validated server-side before the page is even allowed to render; the authenticated user (role,
    permissions, identity) falls out of the same session parse; and the feature-flag service can run
    as a server-side singleton whose rulesets stay fresh in memory, making per-request flag state a
    synchronous, CPU-only computation. The waterfall existed purely because those questions were
    being asked <em>from the browser</em>. So the fix was to stop asking: parse the session once per
    request, compute the flag state against the in-memory rules, and hand all three (Auth0 user,
    authenticated user, flag state) to the providers as props. Each provider initializes
    synchronously from its prop and renders its children immediately: no auth round trip, no
    blocking query, no CDN init, no loading screen. The page pre-renders completely, and
    hard-navigation TTI collapses to the soft-navigation number.
  </p>

