  <p>
    And here the "why keep the client query at all?" question gets its sharpest answer:
    <strong>liveness.</strong>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The bootstrapped user provider's GraphQL query wakes on idle and then <em
      >stays subscribed</em
    >. Because it is a live citizen of the GraphQL cache, automatic invalidation does the refreshing:
    when any mutation during the session touches the user's profile, the cache invalidates the
    relevant typename, the subscribed query refires on its own, and the user in context updates
    everywhere, with no callback, no manual refetch, no wiring (<a
      href='14-minimize-the-wires.html#callbacks'>§14.4</a
    >). The same holds for any bootstrapped GraphQL hook: the server seed buys the complete paint,
    and the idle-woken subscription buys automatic refetch whenever the data changes on the server
    or as a result of a client action. Drop the client query and the bootstrap goes stale; drop the
    bootstrap and the paint goes back behind the loader. The pattern needs both halves.
  </p>

  <h2 id='worked'>6.5 Worked example: the table that outgrew its URL</h2>
  <p>
    Now the case this pattern earns its keep on. A reports table renders a lot of data and carries
    complex filters, free-text search, pagination, and multi-column ordering, a query shape that is
    at times genuinely difficult to capture in a URL. The goal is unchanged from the rest of Part A:
    <strong>a complete first paint</strong>, while still letting everything that depends on an
    interaction refetch after that interaction occurs.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/reports/page.tsx - the server sources the default view</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportsPage</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* The default view's rows, fetched where the data lives (§4). The page
     paints complete: toolbar, filters, rows, pagination, all real. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> rows = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getReportRows</span>(DEFAULT_QUERY);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ReportsTable</span> <span class="tok-prop">bootstrappedRows</span>={rows} <span class="tok-prop">defaultQuery</span>={DEFAULT_QUERY} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      In the pages router this exact move was <code>getServerSideProps</code> handing props to the
      page, which is where the pattern grew up; the App Router version is just a server component
      awaiting the same query. Either way the client component below receives a fully-known first
      view.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ReportsTable.tsx - complete on first paint, client-owned after interactions</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportsTable</span>({ bootstrappedRows, defaultQuery }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Filters, search, sort, page: the rich state that outgrew the URL. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [query, setQuery] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(defaultQuery);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> isIdle = <span class="tok-fn">useIsIdle</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> interacted = query !== defaultQuery;

  <span class="tok-com">/* Paused until the browser is idle OR the user changes the query,
     whichever comes first. Keyed by the query, so every interaction
     refetches exactly this data and nothing else. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data, isValidating } = <span class="tok-fn">useReportRows</span>(query, {
    <span class="tok-prop">pause</span>: !isIdle &amp;&amp; !interacted,
  });
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> rows = data ?? bootstrappedRows;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableContainer</span> <span class="tok-prop">isRefreshing</span>={isValidating}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableToolbar</span> <span class="tok-prop">query</span>={query} <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={setQuery} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Rows</span> <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={rows} /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TableContainer</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Count the states the user never sees: no first-load skeleton (the bootstrap painted real
      rows), no empty-state flash, no toolbar waiting on data it doesn't need. After the first
      interaction the component behaves like any live client table: stale rows stay visible under a
      refresh indicator (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>) while the keyed
      query fetches the new page.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

