  <h2 id='caveat'>6.6 The honest caveat: this is a pages-router pattern at heart</h2>
  <p>
    Be clear-eyed about the lineage.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> Bootstrapping grew up in the pages router, where <code
      >getServerSideProps</code
    > was the only way to put server data in the first paint and the client cache was the only thing
    that could react to an interaction. It still works, and <code>ojl-tracker</code> still runs on it
    well. But the App Router's <em>native</em> answer to "the view changed" is better when you can use
    it: put the state in the URL (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>), and a change
    re-renders <strong>only the affected region on the server</strong>, streaming a fresh RSC
    payload for just what changed (<a href='04-ssr.html#routing'>§4.5</a>) instead of re-running a
    whole page or maintaining a duplicate client data layer. One copy of the data, one place it is
    fetched, shareable URLs, and no twin query to keep honest.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the decision comes in this order. <strong>Default to URL state</strong>; most filter, sort,
    and pagination state fits a URL more comfortably than the old instinct assumes, and it buys
    shareability and back-button behavior for free. <strong>Reach for the bootstrap</strong> when
    the state genuinely won't fit: too rich to serialize, too transient for history, or owned by
    client machinery that can't read the URL. And when you do reach for it, keep the whole pattern:
    server seed, idle-deferred twin, client ownership only after the first interaction. The bootstrap
    without the idle gate is just a page that pays for its data twice at the worst moment; the idle
    gate without the seed is just a slower skeleton.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>URL state first.</strong> Bootstrapping is for state that genuinely won't fit the
          URL, not an alternative to trying.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          When bootstrapping, <strong>the server sources the initial view</strong> and the page
          paints complete: no spinners, no skeletons, nothing waiting on the client.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          The client's twin query <strong>starts paused and wakes on idle</strong>; it never
          competes with the first render, and it validates against the server copy when it runs.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          After the first interaction, <strong>the data is client-owned</strong>: the keyed, live
          query refetches in response to state changes; the bootstrap is never consulted again.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Compare server and client copies with <strong>deep equality before swapping</strong>, so
          an identical response doesn't re-render the subtree.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='imp' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

